course: dictionary
lesson: 6304
title: Moroccan: a word about places and people
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words tell you where something or someone comes from. Moroccan comes from Morocco, a country in north-west Africa, just south of Spain. Many words ending in -an work this way, though not all of them.
explain: When Moroccan describes a thing, it is an adjective. In «Hassan's Moroccan gran», the word tells you which gran he means: the one from Morocco. Try swapping in another country, such as «Hassan's Spanish gran», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: When Moroccan names a person, it is a noun. A Moroccan is someone from Morocco, and the plural is Moroccans. To tell which job the word is doing, ask yourself: is it describing something, or naming someone?
fact: Morocco is a country in north-west Africa, just south of Spain.
fact: The Strait of Gibraltar is the narrow stretch of sea between Morocco and Spain.
fact: Moroccan food includes tagine, couscous and mint tea.
fact: Moroccan tiles and lamps are often used in art and in homes.
fact: «Moroccan-style» means done in the style of Morocco.
exercise: «Hassan's Moroccan gran laughs at his toy truck.» Where is Hassan's gran from? | *Morocco | Scotland | The Netherlands | Greece
exercise: Which of these is a Moroccan food? | Shepherd's pie | Porridge | *Couscous | Fish and chips
exercise: «Ahmed is a Moroccan.» What is Ahmed? | A fish | *A person from Morocco | A town in Spain | A type of tile
test: In «The Moroccan lamp glowed», what kind of word is Moroccan? | A noun | A verb | *An adjective
test: Mina drank Moroccan mint tea with her friends. Which country does the word point to? | *Morocco | Italy | Egypt
exam: Which narrow stretch of sea lies between Morocco and Spain? | *the Strait of Gibraltar | the River Thames | the Sahara Desert | the Atlantic Ocean
